#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521594} :activity/id #uuid "34aeab81-66dd-5a0b-8751-468cd63a1098" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048944 :topic/id #uuid "cdc1c937-d8fa-5ff7-934a-a282eda47b10" :topic/math-academy-id 3855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256f968b-f149-5a64-b5e7-35483cba2ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.40\\pm 0.131\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520844} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4ed279-f589-55a4-9518-10e65b24f391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.52\\pm 0.133\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520942} {:answer/id #uuid "6561e4f2-6b4f-5178-9f7d-90b9b8551b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "400\\pm 0.113\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520993} {:answer/id #uuid "76d7e595-409f-5d32-9911-9eac076c3663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.52\\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521028} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2957a5-69d2-53f1-a6dd-b37bfb959fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.92\\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521028} :answer-field/id #uuid "31fb2a2b-85f8-5174-8f8c-064217a8d0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33652587-e008-57dc-9a3a-905f5c4ae6f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142975" :question/problem "In a particular state, $400$ randomly selected working adults were asked the distance from their home to their workplace. The sample mean distance was $8.52\\,\\text{mi}$ with standard deviation $2.40\\,\\text{mi}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the mean distance from home to work  $\\mu $ for all working adults in this state.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8.52\\,\\text{mi},\\,n=400,\\,s=2.40\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{399,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{399,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=400-1=399$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/82/829fa68b18545ff59ffa702fe4567997d635089edf4e90d713b1c8d817c7959f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{399,0.005}=2.588:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $2.588$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.52 & \\pm 2.588\\cdot \\frac{2.40}{\\sqrt{400}}\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ 8.52 & \\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "10d5d17f-5e05-568d-892a-89daf97c2f8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247effcb-626c-5ae3-bd39-1910118941fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "126\\pm 14.048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520838} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e3e8-d9aa-5bcf-bbe3-dae6af3e90a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99\\pm 8.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521239} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7ad11-1129-5b22-9704-503871bfe339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "171\\pm 15.339" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521251} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26c9b8-8eb6-53aa-bf47-535a91ee6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "252\\pm 16.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521259} {:answer/id #uuid "e14ec158-b428-5af8-9f36-458c932a5ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "252\\pm 14.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521259} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8c2ade-aca3-521a-80e5-387f79992d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a9d87e1-81bc-588a-9941-b4e6843c1f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-142514" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $252,$ and the sample standard deviation is $81,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.984)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=252,\\,n=100,\\,s=81.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.984)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{99,0.025}=1.984.\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b33840d7c98bbf799bdc58dc90d84752b3fa84c36d4a9b122a20ec9401b6823.png)\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}252 & \\pm 1.984\\cdot \\frac{81}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 252 & \\pm 16.070\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29756d50-cfb9-5aeb-ab06-3e56c10fad83" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066610ff-3d82-50b2-b812-1338bebdf1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520772} {:answer/id #uuid "06c2f12e-68a5-528e-9841-ad944de82b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 1.288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520773} {:answer/id #uuid "09003a28-3717-5e7f-9baa-237b32a73563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 7.968" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520778} {:answer/id #uuid "34abe2f4-dc85-5332-b992-cc39f815e618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520873} {:answer/id #uuid "84f0eeaf-ad7b-52c3-b682-813bce3f605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 5.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520778} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d5b3dd-2378-5740-8a14-1eae3756ad16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4a9f9de-52c8-5bcd-a936-99bb91021b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142313" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=64$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $60,$ and the sample standard deviation is $24,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.656)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $63$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=60,\\,n=64,\\,s=24.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.656)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{63,0.005}=2.656.\n$$\n\n![](images/55/5596a286f89b2eed9fd6340c00e77915f84883f2d57d1b342b762143811537aa.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & \\pm 2.656\\cdot \\frac{24}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 60 & \\pm 7.968\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43e3e0c5-16b5-54b6-97d1-904aec98a411" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb32a81-2dc3-50ed-840b-da7ac0d69d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 1.084\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520794} {:answer/id #uuid "311561ec-4176-5648-a42d-bd951c43964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 1.356\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520867} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1d33fe-8836-5ceb-9e21-dde0f49573e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 0.542\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520940} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a617b-52cd-5f5b-a837-7d8fa3497d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521048} {:answer/id #uuid "f3605f82-ef49-559d-a1ce-7456d966fa96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pm 1.084\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521048} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f3a275-b656-5d6f-8b7c-3bd7e106dfd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "969c1777-ec8b-5e40-b43c-413b46fc477f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143028" :question/problem "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $50\\,\\text{mph},$ on a standard test car. A sample of $25$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $174\\,\\text{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Assuming that the stopping distances are normally distributed, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu $ of these tires.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $\\,1.711\\,$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/52/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f75b345d-27d5-5c91-b2b0-43c6291fee8a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d97057b-a0ed-5cd4-a152-34da488534bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.9\\pm 1.959\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520790} {:answer/id #uuid "9a012243-783b-5fcd-a20c-31d094c51f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.1\\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521113} {:answer/id #uuid "a711e61d-7f5c-5a9e-a2ea-31ecb7333dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pm 0.995\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521138} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a652d0-3e8f-5aee-82bd-bf86b31f4084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 1.995\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521207} {:answer/id #uuid "f176a196-bce3-51df-8b5e-2c12622bc68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.1\\pm 2.131\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521113} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce4236eb-539a-5a14-a293-5eda94e5a9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb725276-ca73-5e90-be79-e741b1537c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142976" :question/problem "The amount of hemoglobin in the bloodstream of people living in a particular Colorado community follows a normal distribution. An analysis on a random sample of $16$ people yields a mean hemoglobin concentration of $13.1\\,\\text{g/dl}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean hemoglobin concentration $\\mu $ for all people in the community.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc2ed12d-6e0a-50a2-84a6-8decfa5fc1ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521656}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Unknown Population Variance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520748}]}
